UID:98nAf4pvZ7CKFq3Pcp1m5
SUMMARY:Ionospheric O⁺ Transport to the Inner Magnetosphere: A Global MHD a
	nd Test-Particle Study
DTSTAMP:20260717T061739Z
DTSTART:20260729T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Yiwen Zhu  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defense
	  Department: Physics &amp\; Astronomy  Location: Herman Brown Hall HBH 25
	4  During geomagnetic storms\, ionospheric oxygen (O⁺) can become a signif
	icant constituent of Earth's inner magnetosphere\, whose storm-time partic
	le buildup drives the main-phase depression of the surface magnetic field.
	 The ionosphere supplies this oxygen from two main regions: the dayside po
	lar cusp and the nightside auroral oval. Their relative contribution to th
	e inner magnetosphere\, and how each population is energized as it moves i
	nward\, remain poorly quantified\, in part because the transport spans sca
	les that no single model resolves.  In this thesis\, we follow ionospheric
	 oxygen from both sources to the inner magnetosphere using a global magnet
	ohydrodynamic simulation (GAMERA/MAGE) coupled one-way to a test-particle 
	tracer (CHIMP). Because MAGE couples models spanning a range of scales and
	 regions\, it resolves both the mesoscale bursty bulk flows (BBFs) of the 
	plasma sheet that drive earthward transport and the particle dynamics of t
	he inner magnetosphere. For a real storm\, we compare how cusp and nightsi
	de-auroral oxygen reach the plasma sheet and the inner magnetosphere. With
	in an adiabatic (guiding-center) framework\, we decompose the energy gain 
	along the transport into its Fermi\, betatron\, and E×B contributions and 
	quantify each\, and we characterize where the oxygen departs from adiabati
	c behavior. The two sources follow different paths: cusp oxygen takes the 
	long route through the lobe\, while auroral oxygen enters the closed plasm
	a sheet more directly. We also identify a drop-down channel\, in which mes
	oscale plasma-sheet flows pull cusp oxygen down to the equatorial plasma s
	heet and let it enter the inner magnetosphere more efficiently.  To place 
	the contribution on a quantitative footing\, we weight the test particles 
	by O⁺ outflow fluences computed directly from the simulation's MHD variabl
	es\, include charge-exchange loss\, and compare the modeled oxygen against
	 Van Allen Probes observations. Cusp oxygen enters the inner magnetosphere
	 at higher energy and auroral oxygen at lower energy\, so their relative c
	ontribution depends on the number density of ions each source delivers. Th
	rough two different storms\, we show two cases in which the delivered numb
	ers differ\, giving different relative and absolute contributions of the t
	wo sources to the inner magnetosphere.  Finally\, we return to the cusp it
	self. Predicting where the cusp lies from the upstream solar wind is opera
	tionally useful. We develop a machine-learning model\, gradient-boosted de
	cision trees trained on DMSP spacecraft cusp crossings\, that predicts the
	 cusp location from solar-wind and interplanetary-magnetic-field condition
	s\, offering a fast alternative to a full simulation.  Together\, these ch
	apters connect the ionospheric sources of inner-magnetosphere oxygen to it
	s delivery and energization: how cusp and auroral O⁺ reach the inner magne
	tosphere\, how each is energized along the way\, and how much each contrib
	utes across storms. The cusp machine-learning model complements this with 
	a fast predictor of the cusp location from the upstream solar wind.  Join 
	via Zoom: https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/97738620987?pwd=OmGDYN5xMiERaeL
	kGnHvTsx6K0IIOF.1 (https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/97738620987?pwd=OmGDYN
	5xMiERaeLkGnHvTsx6K0IIOF.1)  (Department : Physics &amp\; Astronomy)\n\nht
	tps://events.rice.edu/event/433274-ionospheric-o-transport-to-the-inner-ma
	gnetosphere-a-
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/433274-ionospheric-o-transport-to-the-inn
	er-magnetosphere-a-
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Herman Brown Hall
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
